{"name":"Bluesy Note","hex":"#7c9ab5","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bluesy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Characteristic of, or similar to, blues music."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bluesy"},{"term":"note","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A symbol or annotation.\nA mark or token by which a thing may be known; a visible sign; a character; a distinctive mark or feature; a characteristic quality.\n\nA mark, or sign, made to call attention, to point out something to notice, or the like; a sign, or token, proving or giving evidence.\nA brief remark; a marginal comment or explanation; hence, an annotation on a text or author; a comment; a critical, explanatory, or illustrative observation.","A mark or token by which a thing may be known; a visible sign; a character; a distinctive mark or feature; a characteristic quality."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To notice with care; to observe; to remark; to heed.","To record in writing; to make a memorandum of."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/note"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blues","blocks":[{"text":"Blues is a music genre and musical form that originated among African Americans in the Deep South of the United States around the 1860s. Blues has incorporated spirituals, work songs, field hollers, shouts, chants, and rhymed simple narrative ballads from the African-American culture. The blues form is ubiquitous in jazz, rhythm and blues, and rock and roll, and is characterized by the call-and-response pattern, the blues scale, and specific chord progressions, of which the twelve-bar blues is the most common. Blue notes, usually thirds, fifths or sevenths flattened in pitch, are also an essential part of the sound. Blues shuffles or walking bass reinforce the trance-like rhythm and form a repetitive effect known as the groove."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blues"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/MaRainey.jpg/500px-MaRainey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/MaRainey.jpg/500px-MaRainey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"American blues singer Ma Rainey (1886–1939), the \"Mother of the Blues\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMaRainey.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Blues_shuffle_in_E.png/500px-Blues_shuffle_in_E.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Blues_shuffle_in_E.png/500px-Blues_shuffle_in_E.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Typical blues shuffle rhythm; playⓘ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlues_shuffle_in_E.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Sahel-Sudan.png/500px-Sahel-Sudan.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Sahel-Sudan.png/500px-Sahel-Sudan.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Sahel (brown) and the Sudan (green)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASahel-Sudan.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/John-avery-lomax1_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-John-avery-lomax1_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/John-avery-lomax1_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-John-avery-lomax1_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Musicologist John Lomax (left) shaking hands with musician \"Uncle\" Rich Brown in Sumterville, Alabama ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJohn-avery-lomax1